        "id": "ch15_1_3-1",
        "text": "Pointer Registers\nPointer registers are great registers to use for buffer overflow exploitation (you\nwill learn more about this topic in the next chapter).\nThe first two on the top of Figure 15.3 are the base pointer and the stack\npointer. These two registers are used for stack operations. The base pointer\npoints to the stack\u2019s base, and the stack pointer points to the top of the stack.\nThe third pointer at the bottom is the instruction pointer, and it points to the\naddress of the next instruction that the CPU needs to execute (keep this one\nin your mind; we will revisit it during Chapter 16, \u201cBuffer/Stack Overflow\u201d).",
